## Introduction
Photoelectrochemical (PEC) water splitting into hydrogen has attracted extensive attention in the exploitation and utilization of sustainable energy.[1-3] The development of efficient and suitable semiconductor systems plays an important role in improving
PEC performance.[4,5] So far, various photoanodes have been developed for PEC water splitting, such as \(\mathsf{T i O}_{2}\) [6] \(\mathsf{W O}_{3}\) [7] \(\mathsf{B i V O}_{4}\) [8] \(\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}\) [9] (oxy)nitrides,[10,11] etc. However, these photoanodes suffer severe charge recombination, poor charge carrier transfer and slow interfacial reaction kinetics.[12,13] Although different approaches such as morphology modulation,[14,15] heterojunction engineering,[16] elemental doping,[17] and catalyst decoration,[18,19] have been used to address these limitations, the intrinsically poor surface reactivity still seriously restricts its solar-to-hydrogen conversion efficiency.
Among them, the loading of oxygen evolution cocatalysts (OECs) is considered an effective solution to improve interfacial reaction kinetics and thus accelerate charge separation and transfer.[20-22] Until now, transition metal (Fe, Co, Ni) oxide and hydroxide have been extensively utilized as cocatalysts for oxygen evolution reaction (OER).[23,24] For instance, NiOCH/FeOCH catalysts were employed as cocatalysts for PEC oxygen evolution reaction of metal oxide photoanodes, extremely reducing the interfacial charge recombination rate and accelerating water oxidation kinetics on the surface of photoelectrodes.[25-27] Moreover, \(\mathsf{C o O}_{\mathsf{x}}\) , \(\mathsf{N i F e O}_{\mathsf{x}}\) , \(\mathsf{F e C o O}_{\mathsf{x}}\) catalysts have been utilized in constructing \(\mathsf{C o O}_{\mathsf{x}}/\mathsf{W O}_{3}\) ,[28] \(\mathsf{N i F e O}_{\mathsf{x}}/\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}\) ,[29] \(\mathsf{F e C o O}_{\mathsf{x}}/\mathsf{B i V O}_{4}\) [30] photoanodes, effectively promoting hole transfer and reducing the overpotential of water oxidation reactions. In recent years, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) materials as cocatalysts (such as: CoNi-MOFs,[31] CoFeMOFs[32]) owing to tunable structures and abundant active sites have been exploited for PEC water splitting. Therefore, it is highly essential to develop and design promising OECs for efficient and stable PEC water oxidation reaction.
Inspired by the advantages of regular crystalline lattices, various structures, high porosity and active sites, metal-organic frameworks have drawn extensive interest, such as gas adsorption, membrane separation engineering, light-emitting diodes and high performance electrocatalysts.[33-35] Especially, zeolitic imidazolate frameworks (ZIFs), a subclass of MOFs, feature high thermal stability and can be melted to form an amorphous molten state above their melting temperature \((\mathsf{T}_{\mathsf{m}})\) before reaching decomposition temperature \((\mathsf{T}_{\mathsf{d}}\) ,where \({\sf T}_{\mathfrak{m}}<\) \(\mathsf{T}_{\mathsf{d}}\) .[36,37] The disorder, enhanced mobility and open architecture of MOF materials in the glass state are beneficial to enhance ionic conductivity and the transport, capture, or release of small ions or molecules.[38] MOF glasses are generally formed via quenching the corresponding liquid MOFs to avoid crystallization.[39] For example, Zn-based ZIF-62 with imidazolate (lm) and benzimidazolate (blm) linkers,[40] exhibits a superior glass-forming ability compared with traditional melt-quenched inorganic glasses.[41,42] Enlightened by the superiority of tunable structure and porosity,[43] metal active centers,[44] well proton
conductivity and optical response[45,46] of MOF glasses and MOF cocatalysts, it is obligatory to develop a MOF glass catalyst as oxygen evolution cocatalyst, which may be of great interest for PEC water splitting applications and beyond.
Herein, we report a MOF glass catalyst synthesized via a melting glass-forming cobalt-based zeolitic imidazolate framework (Co- \(\cdot\mathsf{a}_{\mathsf{g}}\mathsf{Z}\mathsf{I F}\!-\!62)\) . Introducing MOF glass catalysts on various metal oxide (NiO: \(\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}\) \(\mathsf{W O}_{3}\) and \(\mathsf{B i V O}_{4})\) semiconductor substrates coupled with NiO hole transport layer constructs the integrated Co- \(\cdot\mathsf{a}_{\mathsf{g}}\mathsf{Z}\mathsf{I F}\!-\!62/\mathsf{N i O}/\mathsf{M O}\) photoanodes. Co- \(\cdot\mathsf{a}_{\mathsf{g}}\mathsf{Z}\mathsf{I F}\!-\!62/\mathsf{N i O}/\mathsf{M O}\) photoanodes exhibit a significantly enhanced photoelectrochemical water oxidation activity and stability in comparison to pristine MO photoanodes due to the excellent conductivity, stability and open active sites of MOF glass. From experimental analyses and density functional theory calculations, MOF glass catalysts can effectively promote charge transfer and separation, improve carrier mobility, accelerate the kinetics of oxygen evolution reaction, and thus improve PEC performance.
## Results and Discussion
Cobalt-based, zeolitic imidazolate framework (Co-ZIF-62) was synthesized via a mechanochemical process under a high-purity nitrogen environment, which chemical structure and synthesis method are shown in Scheme 1. Especially, the cobalt-based zeolitic imidazolate framework glass (Co- \(\cdot\mathsf{a}_{\mathsf{g}}\mathsf{Z}\mathsf{I F}\!-\!62)\) was achieved by melting glass-forming Co-ZIF-62 crystals. The integrated Co- \(\cdot\mathsf{a}_{\mathsf{g}}\mathsf{Z}\mathsf{I F}\!-\!62/\mathsf{N i O}/\mathsf{M O}\) photoanodes for PEC water splitting were synthesized by electrodeposition of NiO as hole
transport layer (HTL) and spin coating Co- \(\cdot\mathsf{a}_{\mathsf{g}}\mathsf{Z}\mathsf{I F}\!-\!62\) cocatalyst layer on semiconductor substrates.
With regard to the synthesis, Co-ZIF-62 was synthesized through a mechanochemical approach with the replacement of ZnO by \(\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{O A c})_{2}{\cdot}4\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}\) under a high-purity nitrogen environment, resulting into the incorporation of \({\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}\) into the ZIF62 structure (denoted as \(x\%\) Co-ZIF-62). Notably, inductively coupled plasma-optical emission spectrometer (ICP-OES) analyses were used to determine Zn and Co ratios of ZIF-62 crystal and glass samples produced by mechanochemical process (Table S1). X-ray diffraction (XRD) tests were conducted for \(x\%\) Co-ZIF-62 to confirm the crystal structures of
the products. Through the melt-quenching process, no evident Bragg peaks of the Co-a2ZIF-62 are observed when the substitution of \({\mathsf{C}}{\mathsf{o}}^{2+}\) is less than \(10\%\) , indicating the destruction of crystal structure and the formation of an amorphous glassstate material. However, the samples are not fully converted to the glass state with distinct Bragg peaks when the substitution of \({\mathsf{C}}{\mathsf{o}}^{2+}\) is more than \(10\%\) (Figures 1a and S1). This is due to the destruction of the structure of products by the by-product acetic acid when a greater \(\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{O A c})_{2}\cdot4\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}\) addition quantity.[47]
Therefore, \(10\%\) Co-ZIF-62 was chosen as the example for subsequent structural characterization. As shown in the flourier transformed infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) (Figure 1b), the samples retain their chemical bond connection during the mechanical ball milling and vitrification process. The \(\mathsf{C}\mathrm{{=}}\mathsf{O}\) stretching vibration in Co-ZIF-62 at \(1690~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}\) is attributed to a small residual N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF) solvent. After melting quenching process, the residual solvent peak disappears.[48,49] Furthermore, hydrogen nuclear magnetic
resonance \(\left({}^{1}\mathsf{H}\right.\) NMR) was performed to determine the linker ratios of Im and blm in the crystals and glass-state materials (Figures S2~S3). The molar ratios of Im and blm linkers are confirmed to be \(\left(\mathsf{I m}\right)_{1.73}\left(\mathsf{b l m}\right)_{0.27}\) for \(10\%\) Co-ZIF-62 and \(10\%\) Co\(\mathsf{a}_{2}\mathsf{Z}\mathsf{I F}\!-\!62\), indicating negligible thermal decomposition during the process of melting to form glasses. All Co-ZIF-62 materials show the expected Im/blm linker ratio, demonstrating the feasibility of the mechanochemical method to produce specific ZIF structures. Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) and differential scanning calorimetry (DSC) analysis were carried out to confirm the melting behavior of \(10\%\) Co-ZIF-62 prepared by mechanochemical method. Under the \(10\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\,\mathsf{m i n}^{-1}\) heating rate in
\(\mathsf{N}_{2}\), the residual solvent loses away gradually. \(10\%\) Co-ZIF-62 is stable up to \(\sim\!550\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and decomposes slightly subsequently (Figure 1c). \(10\%\) Co-ZIF-62 displays an endothermic signal at \(369.3\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) corresponding to the melting temperature, and the glass transition temperature, \(320.8\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) of \(10\%\) Co- \(\mathsf{a}_{2}\mathsf{Z}\mathsf{I F}\!-\!62\) is determined by the repeated DSC up-scans (Figures 1d,e). The microporous structure of \(10\%\) Co-ZIF-62 crystal and \(10\%\) Co\(\mathsf{a}_{2}\mathsf{Z}\mathsf{I F}\!-\!62\) glass was probed using \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) adsorption isotherms at 298K using Brunauer-Emmett-Teller theory (BET). From Figure 1f, the melt-quenching \(10\%\) Co- \(\mathsf{a}_{2}\mathsf{Z}\mathsf{I F}\!-\!62\) still retains high microporosity and \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) adsorption capacity.
The morphology of mechanochemically synthesized Co-ZIF-62 and Co- \(\cdot\mathsf{a}_{2}\mathsf{Z}\mathsf{I F}\!-\!62\) glass was evaluated by scanning electron microscopy (SEM) and transmission electron microscopy (TEM). Through SEM morphology analyses, mechanochemically
synthesized Co-ZIF-62 particles present a sub-micron bubbly structure and the Co- \(\cdot\mathsf{a}_{2}\mathsf{Z}\mathsf{I F}\!-\!62\) displays a successively sleek morphology after the melt-quenching process (Figure S4), which is obviously smaller and well-proportioned in comparison of Co
ZIF-62 synthesized using the conventional hydrothermal method (Figure S5). As shown in Figure 2b, \(\mathsf{C o-a_{g}Z I F-62}\) covered an fluorinedoped tin oxide (FTO) via vitrification process displays a smooth and continuous morphology in comparison with pristine FTO glass (Figure 2a). Moreover, the TEM image of \(\mathsf{C o-a_{g}Z I F-62}\) shows apparently amorphous state (Figure 2c). The selected-area electron diffraction (SAED) pattern is exhibited in Figure 2d, verifying the formation of amorphous \(\mathsf{C o-a_{g}Z I F-62}\) glass. Then, \(10\%\) \(\mathsf{C o-a_{g}Z I F-62}\) catalyst was chosen as the example coupled with NiO HTL grafted on \(\mathsf{B i V O_{4}}\) substrate to construct an integrated photoanode. After the loading of NiO layer, the surface of \(\mathsf{B i V O_{4}}\) appears shaggy NiO thin nanosheets (Figures 2e,f and S6~S7). TEM image of the \(\mathsf{N i O/B i V O_{4}}\) photoanode reveals that the NiO layer consists of tens of nanometers
nanoparticles (Figure S8). From the high-resolution electron microscope (HR-TEM) image (Figure S9), the lattice fringe of \(0.29\ \mathsf{n m}\) and \(0.24\ \mathsf{n m}\) can be assigned to (040) plane of \(\mathsf{B i V O_{4}}\) and (111) plane of NiO, respectively. After spin-coating of \(\mathsf{C o-a_{g}Z I F-62}\) catalyst onto the surface of \(\mathsf{N i O/B i V O_{4}}\) photoanode, \(\mathsf{C o-a_{g}Z I F-62}\) catalyst is uniformly dispersed on the surface of photoanode (Figures 2g and S10). Moreover, the TEM and HR-TEM images demonstrate that \(\mathsf{C o-a_{g}Z I F-62}\) catalyst compactly attaches on the surface of \(\mathsf{B i V O_{4}}\) nanocrystals (Figures 2h,i), and the type of lattice fringe can be indexed to (040) plane of \(\mathsf{B i V O_{4}}\) in \(\mathsf{C o-a_{g}Z I F-62/B i V O_{4}}\). Of note, the images from the element mapping of energy dispersive spectroscopy (EDS) indicate each element homogeneous distribution of \(\mathsf{C o-a_{g}Z I F-62/N i O/B i V O_{4}}\) (Figures 2j and S11).
After the coupling NiO HTL and \(\mathsf{C o\mathrm{-}a_{2}Z I F\mathrm{-}62}\) catalyst on \(\mathsf{B i V O_{4}}\) photoanode, the XRD patterns of \(\mathsf{B i V O_{4}}\) , \(\mathsf{N i O/B i V O_{4}}\) and \(\mathsf{C o\mathrm{-}a_{2}Z I F\mathrm{-}62/N i O/B i V O_{4}}\) photoanodes are presented in Figure S12. All the diffraction peaks are assigned to monoclinic \(\mathsf{B i V O_{4}}\) (JCPDS PDF #14-0688) and \(\mathsf{S n O}_{2}\) (JCPDS PDF #41-1445). However, no obvious signals for NiO and \(\mathsf{C o\mathrm{-}a_{2}Z I F\mathrm{-}62}\) are observed in the \(\mathsf{C o\mathrm{-}a_{2}Z I F\mathrm{-}62/N i O/B i V O_{4}}\) photoanode, which is possibly owing to the low amount of NiO and the amorphous \(\mathsf{C o\mathrm{-}a_{2}Z I F\mathrm{-}62}\) . In addition, the chemical composition and elemental states of various photoanodes were determined by X-ray photoelectron spectra (XPS). Compared with pristine \(\mathsf{B i V O_{4}}\) , the
slightly positive shift of Bi 4f and V 2p are observed in \(\mathsf{N i O/B i V O_{4}}\) (Figures S13a,b), indicating the certain interaction of NiO and \(\mathsf{B i V O_{4}}\) . From Figure S13d, Ni 2p binding energies appear at 872.8 and 855.2 eV for Ni \(2{\mathsf{p}}_{1/2}\) and Ni \(2{\mathsf{p}}_{3/2}\) respectively, revealing the presence of \(\mathsf{N i}^{2+}\) in NiO.[50] The Ni 2p peak for \(\mathsf{C o\mathrm{-}a_{2}Z I F\mathrm{-}62/N i O/B i V O_{4}}\) shifted about \(0.4\,\mathsf{e V}\) toward a lower binding energy compared with \(\mathsf{N i O/B i V O_{4}}\) , suggesting the intimate interaction between \(\mathsf{C o\mathrm{-}a_{2}Z I F\mathrm{-}62}\) and \(\mathsf{N i O/B i V O_{4}}\) . The peak at \(530.9\,{\mathsf{e V}}\) in the O 1s spectrum suggests that the formation of NiO bond in \(\mathsf{N i O/B i V O_{4}}\) (Figure S13c).[51] In the N 1s XPS spectrum, the peaks located centered at 399.7 and 398.5 eV
can be ascribed to M-N \((\mathsf{M}=\mathsf{C}\mathsf{o}\) , Zn) interaction and \(\mathsf{C}{=}\mathsf{N}{-}\mathsf{C}\) coordination (Figure S14a), and the C 1s XPS spectrum contains two main peaks centered at 288.3 and \(284.4~\mathrm{\eV}\) corresponding to \(\mathsf{N{-}C{=}N}\) coordination and \(\mathsf{s p}^{2}\,\mathsf{C}{=}\mathsf{C}\) bonds in the phenyl group, respectively (Figure S14b).[52] Moreover, in Co 2p XPS spectrum, two peaks at 782.0 and \(797.2\,{\sf e V}\) are ascribed to Co \(2{\mathsf{p}}_{3/2}\) and Co \(2{\mathsf{p}}_{1/2}\) of Co(II) (Figure S14c).[53] Two strong signals centered at 1022.1 and \(1045.3\ \mathrm{eV}\) are identified as Zn \(2{\mathsf{p}}_{3/2}\) and Zn \(2{\mathsf{p}}_{1/2}\) of \(Z\mathsf{n}(\mathsf{I I})\) , respectively (Figure S14d).[54] Thus, the combined analyses of SEM images, XRD and XPS spectra confirm the successful synthesis of the integrated \(\mathsf{C o-a_{9}Z I F-}\) \(62/\mathsf{N i O/B i V O_{4}}\) photoanode.
The PEC performance of \(\mathsf{B i V O}_{4}\) \(\mathsf{C o-a_{9}Z I F-62/B i V O_{4}}\) , and Co\(\mathsf{a}_{9}\mathsf{Z}\mathsf{I F}\!-\!62/\mathsf{N i O/B i V O}_{4}\) photoanodes was evaluated using a three-electrode cell in \(0.5\,\mathrm{~M~}\) KBi electrolyte \((\mathsf{p H}{=}9.5)\) . From linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) tests, \(10\%\) \(\mathsf{C o-a_{9}Z I F-62/B i V O_{4}}\) photoanode delivers the high photocurrent density in comparison to other \(\mathsf{C o-a_{9}Z I F-62/B i V O_{4}}\) photoanodes with different contents of cobalt (Figure 3a). Especially, the activity of \(\mathsf{C o-a_{9}Z I F-62/B i V O_{4}}\) photoanode is significantly higher than CoZIF-62/BiVO4 with the crystal Co-ZIF-62 catalyst (Figure S15). Therefore, we chose \(10\%\) \(\mathsf{C o-a_{9}Z I F-62}\) as the typical catalyst and optimized the loads of \(\mathsf{C o-a_{9}Z I F-62}\) and NiO to explore PEC performances of integrated photoanodes (Figures S16-S17). From Figures 3b,c, after loading NiO HTL, the \(\mathsf{C o-a_{9}Z I F-}\) \(62/\mathsf{N i O/B i V O_{4}}\) photoanode exhibits the highest photocurrent density compared with \(\mathsf{B i V O_{4}}\) \(\mathsf{C o-a_{9}Z I F-62/B i V O_{4}}\) and \(\mathsf{N i O/B i V O_{4}}\) photoanodes. The negligible dark currents of chopped LSV curves suggest that the oxidation photocurrents originate from the surface reaction catalyzed by the photo-generated carriers (Figure 3b). \(\mathsf{B i V O_{4}}\) and \(\mathsf{N i O/B i V O_{4}}\) photoanodes achieve the photocurrent densities of 1.43 and \(2.85\;\mathsf{m A}\;\mathsf{c m}^{-2}\) at \(1.23\;\mathrm{V}\) versus reversible hydrogen electrode (vs. RHE) under AM 1.5G simulated sunlight illumination ( \(100\ m\mathsf{W}\) \(\mathsf{c m}^{-2})\) . After \(\mathsf{C o-a_{9}Z I F-62}\) coated on the surface of photoanodes, \(\mathsf{C o-a_{9}Z I F-62/B i V O_{4}}\) and \(\mathsf{C o-a_{9}Z I F-62/N i O/B i V O_{4}}\) photoanodes reach the photocurrent densities of 3.42 and \(5.34~\mathsf{m A}~\mathsf{c m}^{-2}\) at \(1.23\ \mathrm{~V~}\) vs. RHE respectively. Noteworthily, the photocurrent density of as-synthesized \(\mathsf{C o-a_{9}Z I F-62/N i O/B i V O_{4}}\) photoanode is approximately 3.73 and 1.87 times higher than that of \(\mathsf{B i V O_{4}}\) and \(\mathsf{N i O/B i V O_{4}}\) photoanodes, indicating \(\mathsf{C o-a_{9}Z I F-62}\) catalyst and NiO HTL significantly promote the OER performance.
To elucidate the effect of monochromatic light upon the photocurrent density, Figure 3d shows the wavelength of incident photon to current conversion efficiencies (IPCEs) for pristine \(\mathsf{B i V O_{4}}\) \(\mathsf{C o-a_{9}Z I F-62/B i V O_{4}}\) and \(\mathsf{C o-a_{9}Z I F-62/N i O/B i V O_{4}}\) photoanodes. Over the entire wavelength range, the loading of \(\mathsf{C o-a_{9}Z I F-62}\) leads to significantly enhanced IPCE values compared with the pristine \(\mathsf{B i V O_{4}}\) samples. At \(140\;\mathsf{n m}\) , the IPCE value of \(\mathsf{C o-a_{9}Z I F-62/N i O/B i V O_{4}}\) photoanode reaches up to \(90.8\%\) . From Figure 3e, the maximum applied bias photon to current efficiency (ABPE) for the \(\mathsf{C o-a_{9}Z I F-62/N i O/B i V O_{4}}\) photoanode could reach up to \(1.67\%\) at 0.74V vs. RHE, which is higher than those of \(\mathsf{B i V O_{4}}\) \((0.17\%)\) and \(\mathsf{C o-a_{9}Z I F-62/B i V O_{4}}\) \((1.16\%)\) photoanodes. Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) was employed for investigating the interfacial kinetics of photoanodes during OER process (Figure 3f). The diameter of semi-circles represents the charge transfer resistance (Rct) of electrode-electrolyte interface, which reflects the electron transfer kinetics at the photoanode/electrolyte interface.[55] Rct
values of the \(\mathsf{B i V O_{4}}\) \(\mathsf{C o-a_{9}Z I F-62/B i V O_{4}}\) and \(\mathsf{C o-a_{9}Z I F-}\) \(62/\mathsf{N i O/B i V O_{4}}\) photoanodes are 410.3, 157.6 and \(118.5\ \Omega\) respectively. The EIS measurements reveal that MOF glass Co\(\mathsf{a}_{9}\mathsf{Z}\mathsf{I F}\!-\!62\) catalyst significantly facilitates the interfacial charge transfer and reduces the electron transfer resistance. Moreover, \(\mathsf{C o-a_{9}Z I F-62}\) catalyst and NiO HTL were also deposited on the surface of \(\mathsf{W O}_{3}\) and \(\mathsf{P e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}\) photoanodes (Figures S18-S21). The improved photocurrent densities and reduced charge transfer resistances of as-prepared \(\mathsf{C o-a_{9}Z I F-62/N i O/W O_{3}}\) and \(\mathsf{C o-a_{9}Z I F-62/N i O/F e_{2}O_{3}}\) photoanodes are also achieved, indicating the universal applicability of the \(\mathsf{C o-a_{9}Z I F-62}\) catalyst and NiO HTL (Figures S22-S25). Thus, the integration of Co\(\mathsf{a}_{9}\mathsf{Z}\mathsf{I F}\!-\!62\) catalyst and NiO HTL on these photoanodes could effectively improve the PEC performance.
To further scrutinize the origin of improved charge transport, the Mott-Schottky (M-S) plots of \(\mathsf{B i V O_{4}}\) based photoanodes were investigated at the frequency of \(1\ \mathsf{k H z}\) . As shown in Figure 4a, all M-S curves of \(\mathsf{B i V O_{4}}\) \(\mathsf{C o-a_{9}Z I F-62/B i V O_{4}}\) and \(\mathsf{C o-a_{9}Z I F-}\) \(62/\mathsf{N i O/B i V O_{4}}\) photoanodes exhibit a positive slope indicating the n-type characteristics. Moreover, the \(\mathsf{C o-a_{9}Z I F-62/N i O/B i V O_{4}}\) photoanode exhibits a much smaller slope than that of the \(\mathsf{B i V O_{4}}\) and \(\mathsf{C o-a_{9}Z I F-62/B i V O_{4}}\) photoanodes. The charge carrier densities \((\mathsf{N}_{\mathsf{d}})\) were calculated, such as \(\mathsf{B i V O_{4}}\) \((4.73\!\times\!10^{20})<\mathsf{C o-}\) \(\mathsf{a}_{9}\mathsf{Z}\mathsf{I F}\!-\!62/\mathsf{B i V O}_{4}\) \((6.03\!\times\!10^{20})\) < \(\mathsf{C o-a_{9}Z I F-62/N i O/B i V O_{4}}\) \((8.33\!\times\!10^{20})\) , indicating the introduction of \(\mathsf{C o-a_{9}Z I F-62}\) and NiO HTL enhances electronic conductivity, photogenerated charge transfer and separation efficiency.
\(\mathsf{B i V O}_{4}\) \((33.4\%)\) photoanodes at \(1.23~\mathrm{V}\) vs. RHE. These results indicate that the surface charge transfer efficiency of Co- \(\cdot{\mathsf{a}}_{\mathsf{g}}{\mathsf{Z}}{\mathsf{I F}}.\) 62/NiO/BiVO4 photoanode is obviously improved compared to the pristine \(\mathsf{B i V O4}\) photoanode due to the incorporation of Co\(\mathsf{a}_{2}\mathsf{Z}\mathsf{I F}\!\!-\!62\) catalyst and NiO HTL. The charge storage capacity of NiO as hole transport layer was investigated by the previously reported transient-state photocurrent method based on the cathodic photocurrent peaks measured under chopped illumination.[59,60] When the light is cut off, the trapped holes on surface are reduced, expressing the formation of a cathodic
current (Figures S26–S27). The number of stored holes is represented by the photocurrent integration from the transient state dropped to the steady state.[10,13] The number of holes stored of Co- \(\cdot{\mathsf{a}}_{\mathsf{g}}{\mathsf{Z}}{\mathsf{I F}}{\mathsf{-}}62/{\mathsf{N i O}}/{\mathsf{B i V O}}_{4}\) photoanode is significantly more than \(\mathsf{B i V O4}\) and Co- \(\cdot{\mathsf{a}}_{\mathsf{g}}{\mathsf{Z}}{\mathsf{I F}}{\mathsf{-}}62/{\mathsf{B i V O}}_{4}\) photoanodes especially at minor bias (Figure 4f). These results further verify the strong hole extraction capability of NiO as hole transport layer, promoting the effective photogenerated holes transfer and PEC water oxidation performance.
Intensity-modulated photocurrent spectroscopy (IMPS) was used to further study the charge transfer dynamics on the integrated \(\mathsf{B i V O}_{4}\) based photoanodes, and the effect of Co\(\mathsf{a}_{\mathsf{g}}\mathsf{Z}\mathsf{I F}\!-\!62\) catalyst and NiO HTL on interfacial regulation. Figures 5a-c present the IMPS responses of \(\mathsf{B i V O}_{4}\), Co- \(\cdot{\mathsf{a}}_{9}\) ZIF-62/BiVO4 and Co- \(\cdot{\mathsf{a}}_{9}\) ZIF-62/NiO/BiVO4 photoanodes under various potentials. The two semicircles of the IMPS spectrum correspond to the resistance-capacitance attenuation (lower quadrant) and the competition between charge transfer and recombination (upper quadrant), respectively.[61] A small ratio of charge recombination \((\mathsf{k}_{\mathsf{r e c}})/\) transfer \((\mathsf{k}_{\mathsf{r t r a n s}})\) indicates that charge transfer is faster than charge recombination, which is proportional to the semicircular radius of the upper quadrant.[62] In comparison, the \(\mathsf{B i V O}_{4}\) photoanode shows a larger upper semicircle radius, meaning that serious charge recombination exists on the surface of \(\mathsf{B i V O}_{4}\). Moreover, the radii of upper semicircles do not change significantly with the increase of applied potentials, which indicates that \(\mathsf{B i V O}_{4}\) still has rapid charge recombination even under a high potential. After the loading of Co- \(\cdot{\mathsf{a}}_{9}\) ZIF-62 catalyst and NiO HTL on the \(\mathsf{B i V O}_{4}\) photoanode, the radius of the upper semicircle decreases further and changes obviously with the increase of applied potentials. The Co- \(\cdot{\mathsf{a}}_{9}\) ZIF-62/NiO/BiVO4 photoanode delivers the smallest
radius, suggesting that the charge transfer is accelerated and the charge recombination is effectively inhibited after the introduction of Co- \(\cdot{\mathsf{a}}_{9}\) ZIF-62 catalyst and NiO HTL on the \(\mathsf{B i V O}_{4}\) photoanode.
According to IMPS, the high and low frequency intercepts of the X-axis (imaginary current=0) correspond to the charge transfer efficiency of \(\mathsf{k}_{\mathsf{r t r a n s}}/(\mathsf{k}_{\mathsf{r t r a n s}}+\mathsf{k}_{\mathsf{r e c}})\). Where, the frequency of the upper semicircle vertex \((\mathsf{f}_{\mathsf{m a x}})\) corresponds to the sum of charge transfer rate constants and charge recombination rate constants \((\mathsf{k}_{\mathsf{r t r a n s}}+\mathsf{k}_{\mathsf{r e c}}=2\mathsf{f}_{\mathsf{m a x}})\).[63] Based on the above theory, the values of \(\mathsf{k}_{\mathsf{r t r a n s}}\) and \(\mathsf{k}_{\mathsf{r e c}}\) are easily accessible. As shown in Figures 5d,e, Co- \(\cdot{\mathsf{a}}_{9}\) ZIF-62/NiO/BiVO4 and Co- \(\cdot{\mathsf{a}}_{9}\) ZIF-62/BiVO4 exhibit higher values of \(\mathsf{K}_{\mathsf{t r a n s}}\) and lower values of \(\mathsf{K}_{\mathsf{r e c}}\) than that of pristine \(\mathsf{B i V O}_{4}\) at all applied potentials. Co- \(\cdot{\mathsf{a}}_{9}\) ZIF-62/NiO/BiVO4 has the highest \(\mathsf{K}_{\mathsf{t r a n s}}\) and the lowest \(\mathsf{K}_{\mathsf{r e c}}\) among these samples, indicating that NiO HTL greatly accelerates the charge transfer rate from \(\mathsf{B i V O}_{4}\) to Co- \(\cdot{\mathsf{a}}_{9}\) ZIF-62 and Co- \(\cdot{\mathsf{a}}_{9}\) ZIF-62 catalyst can effectively facilitate the charge transfer to the surface, thus reducing surface recombination. Noteworthily, Co- \(\cdot{\mathsf{a}}_{9}\) ZIF-62/NiO/BiVO4 shows the highest charge transfer efficiency (Figure 5f), which means higher PEC water oxidation performance.[64-66]
The continuous stability of photoanodes is the noteworthy factor to evaluate the practical PEC application. Current-time curves were performed to test the photocurrent stability of the photoanodes. In Figure 6a, the photocurrent density of \(\mathsf{B i V O_{4}}\) photoanode declines rapidly. After loading NiO on \(\mathsf{B i V O_{4}}\) the photocurrent stability is significantly improved compared with the pristine \(\mathsf{B i V O_{4}}\). While the \(\mathsf{C o-a_{g}Z I F-62/N i O/B i V O_{4}}\) photoanode
exhibits the outstanding stability, maintaining about \(93.8\%\) of initial photocurrent density after \(^{20\,\mathrm{~h~}}\) continuous irradiation. From XPS spectra and XRD patterns (Figures S14c,S28), it is noted that the valence of \(\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{I I})\) for \(\mathsf{C o-a_{g}Z I F-62}\) and the crystal structure of \(\mathsf{C o-a_{g}Z I F-62/N i O/B i V O_{4}}\) photoanode do not change after the PEC water splitting process, confirming the excellent stability of the \(\mathsf{C o-a_{g}Z I F-62/N i O/B i V O_{4}}\) photoanode. In addition,
the amounts of hydrogen and oxygen evolved on the Co- \(\mathsf{a}_{9}\mathsf{Z}\mathsf{I F}.\) \(62/\mathsf{N i O}/\mathsf{B i V O}_{4}\) photoanode via PEC water splitting process were measured by gas chromatography. The yields of hydrogen and oxygen increase linearly with irradiation time, reaching 938.8 and \(469.4\;\upmu\mathrm{mol}\) after \(10\;\mathfrak{h}\) irradiation respectively, with a faraday efficiency of about \(97\%\) and a stoichiometric ratio of hydrogen to oxygen of 2:1 at this point (Figure 6b). Based on the above analyses, the possible mechanism of enhanced PEC performance on Co- \(\mathsf{a}_{9}\mathsf{Z}\mathsf{I F}\) -62/NiO/MO photoanode is shown in Figure 6d. For the pristine MO photoanode, the large photogenerated charge transfer resistance and slow surface reaction kinetics result in low PEC performance (Figure 6c). By contrast, the significantly ameliorative PEC performance of Co\(\mathsf{a}_{9}\mathsf{Z}\mathsf{I F}\) -62/NiO/MO photoanode attributes to accelerated charge transfer after introducing NiO as hole transport layer and enhanced interfacial reaction kinetics by the introduction of Co\(\mathsf{a}_{9}\mathsf{Z}\mathsf{I F}\) -62 cocatalyst in PEC system.
## Conclusion
In summary, we reported a MOF glass catalyst through melting glass-forming cobalt-based zeolitic imidazolate framework (Co\(\mathsf{a}_{9}\mathsf{Z}\mathsf{I F}\) -62). This typical glass catalyst was introduced on various metal oxide (MO: \(\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}\), \(\mathsf{W O}_{3}\) and \(\mathsf{B i V O}_{4}\)) semiconductor substrates coupled with NiO hole transport layer, constructing the integrated Co- \(\mathsf{a}_{9}\mathsf{Z}\mathsf{I F}\) -62/NiO/MO photoanodes. Co- \(\mathsf{a}_{9}\mathsf{Z}\mathsf{I F}\)
62/NiO/MO photoanodes exhibit a significantly enhanced photoelectrochemical water oxidation activity and stability in comparison to pristine MO photoanodes due to the excellent conductivity, stability and open active sites of MOF glass. Especially, Co- \(\mathsf{a}_{9}\mathsf{Z}\mathsf{I F}\) -62/NiO/BiVO4 photoanode achieves a photocurrent density of \(5.34~\mathsf{m A}~\mathsf{c m}^{-2}\) at \(1.23~\mathrm{~V~}\) vs. RHE and a remarkably enhanced photostability. The experimental analyses and density functional theory calculations clearly indicate that MOF glass Co- \(\mathsf{a}_{9}\mathsf{Z}\mathsf{I F}\) -62 as oxygen evolution cocatalyst can significantly promote charge transfer and separation, improve carrier mobility, accelerate the kinetics of the interfacial reaction, and thus improve PEC water oxidation performance. This work provides an alternative OECs and in-depth insights upon rational design of integrated photoanodes for promising solar energy conversion.
